id: pm-web-ccie
title: Offline mutation queue is not bound to the originating account or idempotency key
description: "Offline API mutations are stored with method, path, and body but no authenticated user identity or idempotency key. If a browser changes accounts before reconnecting, replay uses the current session; if a response is lost after the server commits, retry can repeat a non-idempotent mutation. The CSRF work in pm-web-413z deliberately repairs token freshness without pretending to solve account ownership or exactly-once delivery."
type: Issue
status: in_progress
priority: 0
tags[4]: idempotency,multi-user,offline,security
created_at: "2026-08-30T21:39:59.317Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-04T18:16:01.566Z"
author: codex
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "Every queued mutation is durably bound to the authenticated user/session that created it; a different account never replays it; unknown legacy ownership is preserved and surfaced for explicit recovery without blocking unrelated owned records; every mutation carries a server-enforced idempotency key so retry after an ambiguous response cannot duplicate state; real browser/service-worker/PostgreSQL tests cover logout/login, account switching, lost responses, restart, queue ordering, and zero data loss."
why_now: The CSRF review proved queue records survive browser sessions and surfaced the adjacent cross-account and ambiguous-retry risks; multi-user production readiness requires explicit ownership and exactly-once semantics.
reviewer: security-review
risk: high
confidence: high
reporter: codex
severity: high
environment: pm-web 2026.8.29 offline service-worker queue
repro_steps: "Queue a mutation while authenticated as user A, change the browser session to user B before reconnecting, then flush; the request is authenticated as B. Separately, let the server commit a non-idempotent mutation but drop the response; the retained record retries without an idempotency key."
expected_result: Queued work remains owned by its creator and ambiguous retries are exactly-once.
actual_result: Replay uses whichever session is current and has no deduplication identity.
affected_version: 2026.8.29
component: Offline mutation queue
customer_impact: "A shared browser can attribute pending work to the wrong user, and a network ambiguity can duplicate mutations."
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-04T18:15:59.802Z",claude-orchestrator,"PR #170: queued mutations are stamped with the broadcast account (persisted session store), target workspace and an idempotency key generated before the first network attempt; foreign, unknown-owner and logged-out records are never replayed or deleted, and surface MUTATIONS_BLOCKED with a visible explicit recovery action that adopts unknown-owner records for the current account (assigning a key first). Server: pm_idempotency_keys claims (account, key) atomically, replays stored outcomes, rejects a key reused for a different request (422), never stores transient 425/429 refusals, answers 409 outcome-unknown instead of re-executing a stale pending key, catches outcome-persist failures, indexes created_at and throttles the retention sweep. Implemented by the pi (GLM-5.3) and codex (gpt-6.1-sol) agents; review round 1 (12 Greptile + CodeQL findings) fixed test-first. Local release:check: 451/451 tests pass."
